al layers, with the steepest degradation in the early-to-mid layers, and I need to weave these numbers into the narrative. Looking at the CKA metrics across different vision encoders and training conditions, I'm seeing that SigLIP with projection maintains the highest similarity scores in both linear and RBF variants, while DINO consistently shows lower values. The rank correlation between CKA and RSA is remarkably high at 0.98, and across the LIBERO-Spatial task, training and instruction effects are minimal—both around 0.01 or less—suggesting these factors don't substantially alter the learned representations.\n\nNow I'm compiling the full experimental evidence from the paper: the SigLIP+Projector achieves an RSA of 0.615, the complete VLA model reaches approximately 0.488 across all four conditions, and DINO sits at 0.388. I've applied six different probing methods across both benchmarks, and the instruction ablation shows virtually no effect at 0.004. The bootstrap confidence interval for the 2×2 matrix includes zero, confirming that neither training nor instructions significantly shift the representational structure.\n\nFor the paper, I'm thinking about framing this around where action-relevant representations originate in vision-language-action models, with a title that captures this central question about the source of these representations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":189,"cache_read_input_tokens":30368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":189},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5f463e76-0b11-4e2b-97f3-bb7dd01573c8","uuid":"2517cf2d-345a-44ec-b796-e75c069fc789"}
